  line=13 phase=commentary text=I’m using the Kit verification workflow because this task requires an implementation plus a machine-checked K proof. I’ll first read its routing and shared proof contract, then inspect the supplied prompt and semantics before choosing the proof shape.
  line=26 phase=commentary text=The workflow confirms that the supplied semantics must remain the execution authority: any proof helper that skips program execution would need a universal connection theorem. I’m now inventorying the exact task and semantics so the spec covers the prompt’s full symbolic domain rather than a bounded set of examples.
  line=55 phase=commentary text=I’ve chosen a direct character scanner. It avoids unsupported regular expressions and keeps the proof over arbitrary finite strings: the loop tracks whether it is still before the first word and whether that first word is exactly a pending `I`. Delimiters close/reset a sentence, and modeled whitespace is handled explicitly through the supplied string semantics.
  line=121 phase=commentary text=Concrete LLVM execution now passes the prompt examples plus boundary cases, including consecutive delimiters, leading whitespace, a standalone `I`, and an `I` occurring later in a sentence. I’m moving to the symbolic definition: one structurally recursive scan summary and one loop circularity over the exact translated loop and call-frame continuation.
  line=151 phase=commentary text=The first symbolic run exposed a precise residual: the program’s one-character `strip()` branch was represented by the semantics’ full trim/reverse term, while my summary used the lower-level whitespace predicate. I’m aligning the summary with that exact fixed-semantics expression; this is a definitional equality only and does not intercept execution.
  line=267 phase=commentary text=The residual has narrowed further to a backend simplification limitation: the fixed semantics leaves the symbolic one-character trim equality opaque inside the branch condition. Before adding any rewrite, I’m checking the equivalence as its own bridge-free K claim; only a successful universal auxiliary claim would justify using that equality as a simplifier.
  line=408 phase=commentary text=The bridge-free comparison claims pass for both whitespace partitions and arbitrary continuations. K rejects operational rules that pattern-match nested function terms, so I’m introducing a constructor-valued alias for the already-computed one-character strip result. Its two exhaustive value cases will also be proved bridge-free; the actual program lookup, method call, control, and state transitions still execute under the supplied semantics.
  line=494 phase=commentary text=The exact connection theorem is running over arbitrary continuation and framed state; that broader context is substantially heavier than the earlier value-only check, but it is the scope the soundness contract requires. If the combined run remains slow, I’ll isolate its four exhaustive cases to identify the costly branch without weakening the theorem.
  line=575 phase=commentary text=The loop circularity now closes with `#Top` over an arbitrary symbolic `IntSeq`; no size bound or example enumeration is involved. The only execution accelerator is the exact `c.strip()` comparison bridge, and its four exhaustive fixed-semantics connection claims already close independently without importing the bridge.
  line=652 phase=commentary text=The diagnostic confirms the entry claim does not consume a sibling loop claim as a lemma; it keeps unrolling even though the loop claim itself is proved. I’m applying the contract’s permitted pattern: keep the proved loop theorem in a bridge-free base definition, then add an exact-context loop bridge only in the entry-proof definition. The bridge will be byte-for-byte scoped to the independently proved loop configuration, including stack, frame pop, state, and continuation.
  line=697 phase=commentary text=The widened loop theorem failed only on normalization of deleting frame key `1` from an otherwise symbolic map; execution and result already matched. I’m adding the standard finite-map fact “remove the explicitly present key, when absent from the frame, yields the frame” as a simplification, with a separate bridge-free K claim over the supplied map semantics before relying on it.
  line=774 phase=commentary text=The bounded trace revealed a real context mismatch: the function reaches the loop with the remaining `If; Return` still packaged as one `Stmts` sequence, while my bridge theorem used two already-separated K continuations. I’m repairing both theorem and bridge to the exact reached continuation; this is precisely the context-containment check Gate A is meant to catch.
  line=820 phase=commentary text=Positive proof execution is complete (`connection-spec.k`, `loop-spec.k`, and `spec.k` each produce `#Top`). I’m now running Gate A’s negative probes: off-by-one result mutation, material loop-body mutation, and a `strip`→`upper` connection mutation. Each must be rejected.
  line=940 phase=commentary text=All three gates now pass. The audit found and repaired one continuation-scope issue before reporting: the loop bridge now matches the actual combined `Stmts` suffix, and its independent base proof covers that exact context. I’m writing the final proof ledger with the model’s witnessed whitespace boundary and the separate CPython evidence called out explicitly.
  line=965 phase=final_answer text=Implemented and proved the task over arbitrary finite symbolic MPY strings. - [solution.py](/workspace/solution.py) and generated [solution.mpy](/workspace/solution.mpy) - [verification.k](/workspace/verification.k) and [spec.k](/workspace/spec.k) - [prove.sh](/workspace/prove.sh) exits 0 - [PROOF.md](/workspace/PROOF.md) reports `VALIDATED` - Connection, loop, and target proofs each produced `#Top` - All mutation probes failed as expected - 42,456 differential cases passed with zero mismatches The documented trust boundary includes MPY’s narrower whitespace model versus CPython. RESULT: KPROVE_PASSED — Every full-domain positive proof printed #Top, exited 0, and Gate B passes for the HumanEval contract.
